To convert Markdown formatted text to HTML in C++, you can use the `markdowncpp` library. Here's a simple function to achieve this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdowncpp.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    markdown::MarkdownParser md(markdown);
    markdown::HtmlRenderer renderer;
    renderer.render(html, md.getAST());
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello World\nThis is a sample.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses the `markdowncpp` library to parse the Markdown text and convert it to HTML. The `MarkdownParser` class is used to convert the Markdown text to an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree), which is then used by the `HtmlRenderer` class to generate the HTML output.

Note that the `markdowncpp` library is a third-party library, so you may need to install it separately. You can find it on [GitLab](https://gitlab.com/rzablock/markdowncpp).

